A restart that WORKED read exactly like one that failed.

(The intended commit message. An auto-commit hook committed this edit as
"update tasks\_vps_restart.ps1" before the real commit ran, so the reasoning is
recorded here instead. Content is unchanged and correct.)

tasks/_vps_restart.ps1 slept a flat 20s before checking. Measured 2026-09-09: the
VPS takes ~25-30s to bind after Start-ScheduledTask, so on a completely successful
restart it printed "NOTHING on 3001" and four "FAIL Unable to connect" lines. The
server was up seconds later on PID 4728, startedAt 2026-09-09T19:51:27.318Z,
serving every one of those four endpoints with http 200.

The only reason the contradiction was visible is that the script's own /api/size
probe, a few lines further down, answered fine against the server it had just
declared dead. Without that accident the reading was "the restart failed" on the
box that trades - which is how a live server gets chased as a dead one, or worse,
restarted a second time underneath itself.

CLAUDE.md already names the mirror image: "a restart that silently no-opped looks
exactly like the code change not working". This is the same defect pointing the
other way, and a fixed sleep can only ever be wrong on one side or the other.

Replaced with the same poll ensure_running.ps1 already uses: /api/status every 3s
up to 90s, then report how long it actually waited. On a genuinely dead server it
now says so after a real 90s instead of implying 20s was ever the answer.

VERIFIED BY RUNNING:
- PSParser tokenize: PARSE OK, 514 tokens, 0 errors
- loop against the live server: "server answered after 3s", real elapsed 4.1s -
  returns on the first poll instead of blocking for 20
- loop against a port with nothing on it: "correctly reported down after 9s" -
  gives up cleanly rather than hanging
- still ASCII apart from the pre-existing line-1 em-dash, which was not touched;
  the sibling script's header warns PS 5.1 reads a .ps1 as ANSI without a BOM

Touches nothing on the signal path: no gate, threshold, confidence, setup, lot,
stop or schedule. Cannot block a signal or the learning writers - it only changes
how long this script waits before printing what it found.

